const e={language:"Hebrew",title:"The Letters of Creation",tagline:"Eight lessons from the shape of the aleph to the Shema and the first mishnah of Sefer Yetzirah.",overview:"This course assumes nothing. It begins with a line of letters running the wrong way across the page and ends with you reading two of the shortest and heaviest sentences in the tradition: the Shema of Deuteronomy 6:4, and the opening mishnah of Sefer Yetzirah. Between them you will learn the twenty-two letters and their five finals, the vowel points and why the Torah scroll does without them, the three-letter root on which the lexicon hangs, the prefixes hidden in the first word of the Bible, the construct chain by which Hebrew says the X of Y, and the reckoning of letters as numbers. It will not make you fluent: the verb system is barely opened, the cantillation untouched, the Aramaic of the Zohar outside it altogether. What it gives you is the ability to open a Hebrew page, take a word apart, and see what a translation has silently decided for you.",lessons:[{number:1,title:"Twenty-Two Servants",aim:"Read the alphabet right to left, tell apart the letters that deceive the eye, master the five final forms, and know honestly what hand the Torah scroll is written in.",sections:[{heading:"Reading against the current",paragraphs:["The first difficulty is not the letters but the direction. Your eye has spent its life beginning at the left margin and will keep trying to. Train it deliberately: find the right edge, start there, and read short words aloud until the direction costs you nothing.","All twenty-two letters are consonants. Three of them — he, vav and yod, with aleph in some positions — do part-time duty as vowel markers, the matres lectionis, mothers of reading. A Hebrew word is a consonantal skeleton and the reader supplies the flesh from knowledge. Each letter also carries a number, one to four hundred, and a name that is an ordinary noun — aleph an ox, bet a house. The tradition treats none of this as accident."],examples:[{native:"אב",translit:"ʾav",gloss:"father",note:"Aleph, then bet. Start at the right."},{native:"יד",translit:"yad",gloss:"hand",note:null},{native:"שם",translit:"shem",gloss:"name",note:"Shin, then final mem."}]},{heading:"The pairs that deceive the eye",paragraphs:["Write the letters; do not merely look at them. The work is in the pairs beginners conflate. Bet has a square heel at the bottom right where kaf is rounded; dalet is cornered at the top right where resh curves; he has a gap at the top left where chet is closed. Vav, zayin and final nun are three uprights distinguished by head and descent; samekh is round, final mem square. This is no curiosity: in the Shema the dalet of echad is written large, so that no reader can mistake echad, one, for acher, another."],examples:[{native:"ב כ",translit:"b / k",gloss:"bet and kaf — find the square heel",note:null},{native:"ד ר",translit:"d / r",gloss:"dalet and resh — the top right corner",note:null}]},{heading:"Five letters that change at the end",paragraphs:["Five letters take a second shape at the end of a word: kaf, mem, nun, pe and tsadi. Four of them straighten and drop below the line, כ becoming ך; mem instead closes into a square, ם. Set side by side the five final shapes run ךםןףץ. The grammarians remember the five by the acronym מנצפ״ך, mantzpakh, built from the ordinary forms of the same five letters; the Talmud discusses these forms under that name at Megillah 2b. They are not extra letters and carry no extra value: a final mem is worth forty, exactly as an ordinary mem is.","In printed Hebrew the rule is positional and absolute. One honest qualification: the distinction hardened over time, and in the Judaean Desert manuscripts the two shapes are sometimes used interchangeably. What you learn here is the settled Masoretic and printed convention."],examples:[{native:"מלך",translit:"melekh",gloss:"king",note:"Final kaf."},{native:"ארץ",translit:"ʾerets",gloss:"land, earth",note:"Final tsadi."},{native:"שלום",translit:"shalom",gloss:"peace, wholeness",note:"Final mem."},{native:"יוסף",translit:"Yosef",gloss:"Joseph",note:"Final pe."}]},{heading:"Two hands",paragraphs:["These letters are ketav ashuri, the square script — an Aramaic hand adopted after the Babylonian exile. Before it Hebrew used paleo-Hebrew, a descendant of the Phoenician alphabet, the hand of the Siloam inscription and the Lachish ostraca. At Sanhedrin 21b–22a the sages disagree: one view holds the Torah was given in ketav ivri and the script changed in Ezra's day, another that it was given in ashurit, abandoned, and later restored. The sources disagree, and you should decline to pretend otherwise. The material evidence shows the square script is post-exilic, and that at Qumran both hands were in use at once, some scribes writing the Tetragrammaton in paleo-Hebrew inside a square-script scroll."],examples:[]}],vocabulary:[{native:"אב",translit:"ʾav",gloss:"father"},{native:"בן",translit:"ben",gloss:"son"},{native:"שם",translit:"shem",gloss:"name"},{native:"מלך",translit:"melekh",gloss:"king"},{native:"ארץ",translit:"ʾerets",gloss:"land, earth"},{native:"שלום",translit:"shalom",gloss:"peace, wholeness"},{native:"אמת",translit:"ʾemet",gloss:"truth"},{native:"ספר",translit:"sefer",gloss:"book, scroll"}],exercises:[{prompt:"Name the letters of ארץ right to left, and say why the last is written ץ.",answer:"Aleph, resh, tsadi. The tsadi takes its final form ץ because it stands at the end of the word. Erets, 'land, earth'.",hint:null},{prompt:"Which five letters take a final form? Give both shapes of each, and the acronym by which they are remembered.",answer:"Kaf כ ך, mem מ ם, nun נ ן, pe פ ף, tsadi צ ץ. The five final shapes together are ךםןףץ, and the acronym is מנצפ״ך, mantzpakh, written with the ordinary forms of those five letters.",hint:null},{prompt:"How many letters are there in בראשית, and what is the last?",answer:"Six: bet, resh, aleph, shin, yod, tav. The last is tav, which is also the last letter of the alphabet.",hint:null},{prompt:"A student reads אחר where the text has אחד. Which letters has he confused?",answer:"Dalet ד and resh ר. The dalet has a square corner at its top right where the resh is rounded. The slip turns 'one' into 'another' — which is why the scribal tradition writes the dalet of echad large in the Shema.",hint:null},{prompt:"What script preceded ketav ashuri, and what does the Talmud say about the change?",answer:"Paleo-Hebrew, ketav ivri, a descendant of the Phoenician alphabet. At Sanhedrin 21b–22a the sages disagree: one view says the Torah was given in ketav ivri and changed to ashurit in Ezra's time, another that it was given in ashurit, changed, and later restored. The Talmud does not settle it.",hint:null}]},{number:2,title:"The Breath Between the Letters",aim:"Read the six vowel points you will meet most often, recognise the dagesh, and understand why the Torah scroll carries none of them.",sections:[{heading:"Where the points came from",paragraphs:["The consonantal text of the Bible is very old; the marks that tell you how to pronounce it are not. Between roughly the seventh and tenth centuries the Masoretes — the men of the masorah, the transmission — devised points to fix in writing a reading until then carried by voice alone. The Tiberian system of Ben Asher's school won; its great witnesses are the Aleppo Codex, of about 930, and the Leningrad Codex of 1008 or 1009.","The points record a pronunciation and also interpret it, since to point a word is to decide which word it is. Where points and consonants pulled apart the Masoretes kept both: the ketiv, what is written, and the qere, what is read. The dagesh, a dot inside a letter, hardens bet, gimel, dalet, kaf, pe and tav — ב with the dot is b, without it v — and elsewhere marks a doubled consonant."],examples:[]},{heading:"Six marks to know",paragraphs:["Almost every vowel is written beneath its own consonant and sounded after it, so a mark under the bet is heard after the b. Patach, a horizontal stroke, sounds a. Qamats, a patach with a tail, sounds a in Sephardi and Israeli reading, aw or o in Ashkenazi, and in certain closed unstressed syllables is a true short o, qamats qatan. Chirik, a single dot, sounds i; with a following yod it is chirik male, written full.","Cholam is a dot above and to the left, sounding o; over a vav, cholam male. Shuruk is not a point at all but a vav with a dot in its belly, sounding u. Sheva, two dots stacked, is either a hurried half-vowel like the e in below or nothing whatever — sheva na and sheva nach, and telling them apart is a matter of rules you may postpone. Two more appear below: tsere, e as in they, and segol, e as in bed. One oddity: after a non-a vowel, a word ending in chet, ayin or he-with-a-dot takes a furtive patach, sounded before that letter — רוּחַ is ruach, not ru-cha."],examples:[{native:"בַּת",translit:"bat",gloss:"daughter",note:"Patach; the bet also has a dagesh."},{native:"דָּבָר",translit:"davar",gloss:"word, thing",note:"Qamats twice."},{native:"אִישׁ",translit:"ʾish",gloss:"man",note:"Chirik male, with a yod."},{native:"אוֹר",translit:"ʾor",gloss:"light",note:"Cholam male: the dot above the vav."},{native:"רוּחַ",translit:"ruaḥ",gloss:"spirit, breath, wind",note:"Shuruk: the dot inside the vav."},{native:"שְׁמַע",translit:"shemaʿ",gloss:"hear!",note:"Sheva, then patach."},{native:"אֶחָד",translit:"ʾeḥad",gloss:"one",note:"Segol, then qamats."},{native:"בְּרֵאשִׁית",translit:"bereʾshit",gloss:"in the beginning",note:"Sheva, tsere, chirik male."}]},{heading:"Why the scroll has none",paragraphs:["A Torah scroll carries bare consonants: no vowels, no accents, no verse divisions. Halakhah requires it, and the requirement preserves something true — the written text and the received reading are two distinct deposits of the tradition. The scroll carries the letters; the reader carries the voice, and prepares beforehand from a pointed book.","Feel the stakes in dalet, bet, resh. Unpointed they are simply דבר. Pointed they may be davar, a word; dever, a plague; dibber, he spoke; or dabber, speak! Four words, one spelling. Learn the points, then learn to see past them: when a kabbalist repoints a word to yield a second sense he is not violating the letters, for the letters permitted both readings all along."],examples:[{native:"דָּבָר",translit:"davar",gloss:"a word, a thing",note:null},{native:"דֶּבֶר",translit:"dever",gloss:"plague",note:null},{native:"דִּבֶּר",translit:"dibber",gloss:"he spoke",note:null},{native:"דַּבֵּר",translit:"dabber",gloss:"speak!",note:"One spelling, four words."}]}],vocabulary:[{native:"אוֹר",translit:"ʾor",gloss:"light"},{native:"רוּחַ",translit:"ruaḥ",gloss:"spirit, breath, wind"},{native:"דָּבָר",translit:"davar",gloss:"word, thing"},{native:"אִישׁ",translit:"ʾish",gloss:"man"},{native:"מַיִם",translit:"mayim",gloss:"water"},{native:"אֶחָד",translit:"ʾeḥad",gloss:"one"},{native:"שֵׁם",translit:"shem",gloss:"name"},{native:"נִקּוּד",translit:"niqqud",gloss:"pointing, vocalisation"}],exercises:[{prompt:"Which two of the six vowels are written with the letter vav, and how do they differ?",answer:"Cholam male, וֹ, where the dot rides above and to the left of the vav, sounding o; and shuruk, וּ, where the dot sits inside the body of the vav, sounding u.",hint:"One dot above, one dot within."},{prompt:"Read בְּרֵאשִׁית aloud and name each vowel in order.",answer:"Bereshit. Sheva under the bet, which also carries a dagesh; tsere under the resh; chirik under the shin, written full with a yod. The aleph carries no vowel of its own, and the final tav closes the syllable.",hint:null},{prompt:"Give four ways of pointing דבר, with their meanings.",answer:"Davar, 'word, thing'; dever, 'plague'; dibber, 'he spoke'; dabber, 'speak!'.",hint:null},{prompt:"Why does a Torah scroll carry no vowel points?",answer:"Because halakhah requires the scroll to preserve the consonantal text alone. The points are a later Masoretic notation of an orally carried reading; keeping them out keeps the written text and the received reading distinct.",hint:null},{prompt:"Why is רוּחַ not pronounced ru-cha?",answer:"Because the patach under the final chet is a furtive patach: after a non-a vowel, a word ending in chet, ayin or he-with-a-dot takes a patach sounded before that consonant, not after it.",hint:null}]},{number:3,title:"The Three-Letter Root",aim:"Understand the shoresh, work two roots through their derivatives, and see why every Hebrew dictionary — and every kabbalistic word-play — is organised by root.",sections:[{heading:"Three consonants, and a field of meaning",paragraphs:["A Hebrew word is a root fitted into a pattern. The root, shoresh, is three consonants carrying a field of meaning and no grammar at all; the pattern — vowels, prefixes, suffixes — supplies the grammar. See the root through the pattern and the vocabulary collapses into a few thousand items.","Take kaf-tav-bet, the root of writing. Katav is he wrote; ketav, the same letters differently voweled, is writing or script, the word in ketav ashuri. Mikhtav, with a prefixed mem, is a letter or missive, the mem-pattern making nouns of instrument and place. Ketubah is the marriage contract; Ketuvim, the Writings, is the third division of the Tanakh. Only k, t, v are constant — which is why a consonantal script suits the language so well: the letters on the page are exactly the part that does not change."],examples:[{native:"כתב",translit:"katav",gloss:"he wrote",note:null},{native:"כתב",translit:"ketav",gloss:"writing, script",note:"Same consonants, different word."},{native:"מכתב",translit:"mikhtav",gloss:"a letter, missive",note:"Prefixed mem: the thing written."}]},{heading:"sh-m-r, and the patterns that clothe a root",paragraphs:["Shin-mem-resh means to keep, guard, watch over. Shamar is he kept; shomer, a keeper; shemirah, the act of guarding; mishmeret, a watch or charge; shamor, keep! Verbs are further sorted into seven patterns called binyanim, buildings — qal, nifal, piel, pual, hifil, hufal, hitpael. You need not conjugate them, only know they exist, so that a familiar root in unfamiliar dress sends you looking for the root.","One pair shows what the root buys you. The Sabbath commandment is given twice: Exodus 20:8 says zakhor, remember; Deuteronomy 5:12 says shamor, keep. Rashi, commenting on Exodus 20:8, reports the tradition that the two were spoken in a single utterance — a teaching available only to a reader who sees them as imperatives of two roots standing in the same slot of the same sentence. The hymn Lekhah Dodi opens by repeating it."],examples:[{native:"שמר",translit:"shamar",gloss:"he kept, guarded",note:null},{native:"שומר",translit:"shomer",gloss:"a keeper, guard",note:null},{native:"שמור",translit:"shamor",gloss:"keep!",note:"Deuteronomy 5:12."},{native:"זכור",translit:"zakhor",gloss:"remember!",note:"Exodus 20:8, in the same slot."}]},{heading:"Why the lexicon hangs on roots",paragraphs:["Every serious Hebrew dictionary is arranged by root: to find mikhtav you strip the mem and look under kaf-tav-bet. The doctrine that the root is triliteral was established by Judah ben David Hayyuj at Cordoba around the end of the tenth century, writing in Arabic. But not every word yields a clean triliteral: roots with aleph, he, vav, yod or nun behave irregularly, and particles and pronouns stand outside the system. A listed root is an analysis, not a fact read off the page.","Samekh-pe-resh gives sefer, a book; sofer, a scribe; mispar, a number; sippur, a telling. Sefer Yetzirah says God created His world with three sefarim — sefer, sefar and sippur. That sentence is a root-play, unreadable without the root, and the same root is the traditional source of sefirah; an older derivation from the Greek sphaira is now generally set aside. A caution: roots make word-play cheap, and words sharing three consonants may be genuinely cognate or may merely collide. The tradition is often making a homiletic point rather than an etymological claim."],examples:[{native:"ספר",translit:"sefer",gloss:"book, writing",note:null},{native:"סופר",translit:"sofer",gloss:"scribe",note:null},{native:"מספר",translit:"mispar",gloss:"number",note:null},{native:"סיפור",translit:"sippur",gloss:"a telling, a story",note:"Spelled ספור in older texts."},{native:"ספירה",translit:"sefirah",gloss:"a counting; in kabbalah, an emanation",note:"Traditionally from this root."}]}],vocabulary:[{native:"שורש",translit:"shoresh",gloss:"root"},{native:"כתב",translit:"katav",gloss:"he wrote"},{native:"שמר",translit:"shamar",gloss:"he kept, guarded"},{native:"מכתב",translit:"mikhtav",gloss:"a letter, missive"},{native:"ספר",translit:"sefer",gloss:"book, scroll"},{native:"סופר",translit:"sofer",gloss:"scribe"},{native:"מספר",translit:"mispar",gloss:"number"},{native:"ספירה",translit:"sefirah",gloss:"counting; emanation"}],exercises:[{prompt:"Give the root of מכתב and say what the mem is doing.",answer:"The root is kaf-tav-bet, k-t-v, 'write'. The mem belongs to the pattern, not the root: the mem-pattern forms nouns of instrument or place, so mikhtav is the thing written — a letter.",hint:null},{prompt:"Which of these does not belong to the root sh-m-r: שמירה, שומר, שמים, משמרת?",answer:"שמים, shamayim, 'heavens'. Its consonants are shin, mem, yod, final mem — there is no resh in it at all. The other three show shin, mem and resh in order.",hint:"Look for the resh."},{prompt:"Unpointed, כתב is at least two different words. Give both.",answer:"Katav, 'he wrote', and ketav, 'writing, script', as in ketav ashuri. Only the vowels distinguish them, and the scroll supplies no vowels.",hint:null},{prompt:"Name the three sefarim of Sefer Yetzirah and the root they share.",answer:"Sefer, sefar and sippur — writing, number and telling. All three are built on samekh-pe-resh, the root that also gives sofer, mispar, and traditionally sefirah itself.",hint:null},{prompt:"Who established the doctrine that the Hebrew root is triliteral, and when?",answer:"Judah ben David Hayyuj, at Cordoba around the end of the tenth century, writing in Arabic. Earlier grammarians treated many roots as two-lettered; Hayyuj showed the missing consonant was a weak letter that had dropped from view.",hint:null}]},{number:4,title:"The Little Words in Front",aim:"Recognise the prefixes ha-, ve-, be-, le- and mi-, take the first word of the Torah apart, and meet the vav that turns a Hebrew verb into past narrative.",sections:[{heading:"Five little words that are not words",paragraphs:["Hebrew does not write its smallest words separately. The article, the conjunction and, and three common prepositions attach directly to the following word, with no space and no hyphen. A word that looks four letters long may be a two-letter word wearing two prefixes, and until you can strip them you will find nothing in a dictionary.","The article is ha-, the; there is no indefinite counterpart, since Hebrew has no word for a. In pointing it doubles the following consonant, so ha-shamayim sounds hash-shamayim; before the gutturals aleph, ayin and resh, which will not double, it lengthens its own vowel instead, and before he and chet the pointing usually stays as it is and the doubling is only notional. The conjunction ו is ve-, or u- before bet, vav, mem and pe and before a sheva, or va- in certain positions — unpointed, always simply ו. Three prepositions attach the same way, remembered as bet, kaf, lamed: be-, in; ke-, like; le-, to, for. The fifth prefix, mi- or me-, is from, a squeezed min whose nun assimilates into the next letter and doubles it.","One consequence matters: when the article follows one of these prepositions its he vanishes and its vowel passes to the prefix, be- plus ha-sefer becoming ba-sefer. Unpointed, in a book and in the book are the same four letters."],examples:[{native:"הארץ",translit:"ha-ʾarets",gloss:"the earth",note:null},{native:"ביום",translit:"be-yom",gloss:"in the day",note:"Genesis 2:4."},{native:"ממצרים",translit:"mi-Mitsrayim",gloss:"from Egypt",note:"The nun of min has assimilated."},{native:"בספר",translit:"be-sefer / ba-sefer",gloss:"in a book / in the book",note:"The same four letters."}]},{heading:"Reading בראשית",paragraphs:["Strip the prefixed bet from the Torah's first word and ראשית remains — reshit, beginning or first, built on ראש, rosh, head, the beginning being the head of a thing, as the New Year is Rosh ha-Shanah. So bereshit is be- plus reshit: in beginning.","In beginning, not in the beginning: there is no article, and Hebrew could have written ba-reshit. The definite the of every English Bible is the translator's. Rashi seized on it — a noun without an article standing before a verb suggested a construct chain, so that the opening should read when God began to create. The Septuagint and Vulgate took the absolute sense, and modern versions divide. The ambiguity is genuinely in the Hebrew; it is not a translator's failure."],examples:[{native:"ראש",translit:"roʾsh",gloss:"head",note:null},{native:"ראשית",translit:"reʾshit",gloss:"beginning, first, first-fruits",note:null},{native:"בראשית",translit:"bereʾshit",gloss:"in beginning",note:"be- + reshit. No article."},{native:"בשלשים",translit:"bi-shloshim",gloss:"with thirty",note:"The same bet opens Sefer Yetzirah."}]},{heading:"The vav that turns time",paragraphs:["One more use of the vav must be flagged, because Genesis 1:3 depends on it. Biblical Hebrew's two chief verb forms, perfect and imperfect, correspond only loosely to our past and future. Prefix a vav to an imperfect at the head of a narrative clause and the sense flips: what looks like he will say becomes and he said. This is the vav-consecutive, vav ha-hippukh, the vav of reversal, and it is the engine of biblical narrative. Recognise the shape — a vav, then a prefix letter, most often yod — and you know you are in past narrative."],examples:[{native:"יהי",translit:"yehi",gloss:"let there be",note:"A jussive of the root h-y-h."},{native:"ויאמר",translit:"va-yoʾmer",gloss:"and he said",note:"Genesis 1:3."},{native:"ויהי",translit:"va-yehi",gloss:"and there was",note:null}]}],vocabulary:[{native:"ה",translit:"ha-",gloss:"the (prefixed article)"},{native:"ו",translit:"ve-, u-, va-",gloss:"and"},{native:"ב",translit:"be-, bi-",gloss:"in, at, with, by"},{native:"ל",translit:"le-, li-",gloss:"to, for, belonging to"},{native:"מ",translit:"mi-, me-",gloss:"from"},{native:"יום",translit:"yom",gloss:"day"},{native:"ראש",translit:"roʾsh",gloss:"head"},{native:"ראשית",translit:"reʾshit",gloss:"beginning, first"}],exercises:[{prompt:"Take והארץ, the first word of Genesis 1:2, apart into its pieces.",answer:"Three pieces: ve-, 'and'; ha-, 'the'; and arets, 'earth, land'. Ve-ha-arets, 'and the earth'.",hint:"Two prefixes, then a word from Lesson 1."},{prompt:"Unpointed, בספר can be read two ways. What are they, and what has happened to the letters?",answer:"Be-sefer, 'in a book', and ba-sefer, 'in the book'. When the article follows be-, ke- or le-, its he drops out and its vowel passes to the prefix, so nothing of the article survives among the consonants.",hint:"Where did the he go?"},{prompt:"Why is there no word for 'the' in בראשית, and what did Rashi make of that?",answer:"The word is be- plus reshit, 'in beginning'; had the article been meant, the text could have written ba-reshit. Rashi took its absence as a sign that reshit stands in construct, reading 'When God began to create'. The Septuagint and Vulgate took the absolute sense. The Hebrew permits both.",hint:null},{prompt:"How would you write 'from Egypt', and what happens to the preposition?",answer:"ממצרים, mi-Mitsrayim. The preposition is min; its nun assimilates into the following consonant and doubles it, so only the mem remains visible as a prefix.",hint:null},{prompt:"In ויאמר, what are the first two letters doing?",answer:"The vav is the conjunction 'and'; the yod is the prefix of the imperfect yomer, 'he will say'. Joined as a vav-consecutive they yield past narrative: 'and he said'.",hint:null}]},{number:5,title:"Smichut, the Construct Chain",aim:"Read the commonest structure in the language — the X of Y — and see why kabbalistic Hebrew is built out of chains of it.",sections:[{heading:"The X of Y, without a word for 'of'",paragraphs:["Hebrew has no word for of. To say the word of a king it sets the two nouns side by side and marks the first as leaning on the second. The construction is smichut, leaning; the first noun is called the construct, the second the absolute. Devar melekh, word-of king, stands in the Bible at Ecclesiastes 8:4.","Three things happen to the first noun. It often shortens a vowel, davar becoming devar. A feminine noun ending in he changes that ending to tav, torah becoming torat, as in torat Moshe at Joshua 8:31. And a masculine plural drops its final mem, banim becoming bnei. Unpointed the vowel change is invisible, but the he turning to tav and the mem falling away are plain on the page — your best signal that a chain has begun.","The fourth rule matters most and is most often broken. The article never attaches to the first noun: definiteness is carried by the last and spreads backwards over the whole. So ets chayim is a tree of life, at Proverbs 3:18, and ets ha-chayim the tree of life, at Genesis 2:9 — the article standing on chayim yet defining both words. A chain may run to three links, as in sefer torat Moshe, again at Joshua 8:31, and it also serves as a superlative: shir ha-shirim, the greatest of songs."],examples:[{native:"בני ישראל",translit:"bnei Yisraʾel",gloss:"the children of Israel",note:"Banim loses its final mem."},{native:"תורת משה",translit:"torat Mosheh",gloss:"the Torah of Moses",note:"Joshua 8:31; the he becomes tav."},{native:"עץ חיים",translit:"ʿets ḥayyim",gloss:"a tree of life",note:"Proverbs 3:18 — no article."},{native:"עץ החיים",translit:"ʿets ha-ḥayyim",gloss:"the tree of life",note:"Genesis 2:9 — the article defines both."},{native:"שיר השירים",translit:"shir ha-shirim",gloss:"the Song of Songs",note:"A construct used as superlative."},{native:"על פני",translit:"ʿal penei",gloss:"upon the face of",note:"Penei, construct plural of panim; as in al penei tehom, upon the face of the deep, at Genesis 1:2."}]},{heading:"Chains of the Names",paragraphs:["Much of this hall's vocabulary is smichut. Ruach Elohim, the spirit of God. Elohei Yisrael, the God of Israel. Sefer Yetzirah — the title itself is a chain. Maaseh bereshit and the merkavah are the subjects Mishnah Chagigah 2:1 restricts to the fit and the few. Ein Sof repays a moment: ein is the construct of ayin, there is not, and sof is end, so the phrase says without end. It is a coinage of the Provençal and Geronese kabbalists of the twelfth and thirteenth centuries, not a biblical term.","The chain is also where interpretative freedom lives. Because it juxtaposes two nouns and leaves the relation unstated, X of Y may mean belonging to Y, made of Y, characterised by Y, or acting upon Y. Ruach Elohim may be the spirit of God, a wind from God, or — Elohim serving as a superlative — a mighty wind. All three are defensible and all three are printed. When you next read a translation of Genesis 1:2, notice that it has decided for you."],examples:[{native:"רוח אלהים",translit:"ruaḥ ʾElohim",gloss:"the spirit of God; a mighty wind",note:"Genesis 1:2, genuinely ambiguous."},{native:"אלהי ישראל",translit:"ʾElohei Yisraʾel",gloss:"the God of Israel",note:"Elohim in construct loses its mem."},{native:"מעשה בראשית",translit:"maʿaseh bereʾshit",gloss:"the work of creation",note:"Mishnah Chagigah 2:1."},{native:"אין סוף",translit:"ʾEin Sof",gloss:"the Infinite, 'without end'",note:"A medieval kabbalistic coinage."},{native:"נתיבות פליאות חכמה",translit:"netivot peliʾot ḥokhmah",gloss:"wondrous paths of wisdom",note:"Sefer Yetzirah 1:1 — three links."}]}],vocabulary:[{native:"דבר",translit:"davar (constr. devar)",gloss:"word, thing"},{native:"עץ",translit:"ʿets",gloss:"tree"},{native:"חיים",translit:"ḥayyim",gloss:"life"},{native:"בית",translit:"bayit (constr. beit)",gloss:"house"},{native:"מעשה",translit:"maʿaseh",gloss:"work, deed"},{native:"סוף",translit:"sof",gloss:"end"},{native:"נתיב",translit:"nativ (pl. netivot)",gloss:"path"},{native:"יצירה",translit:"yetsirah",gloss:"formation, fashioning"}],exercises:[{prompt:"Translate עץ חיים and עץ החיים, and explain the difference.",answer:"'A tree of life' (Proverbs 3:18) and 'the tree of life' (Genesis 2:9). The article never attaches to the first noun of a chain; definiteness is carried by the last and spreads over the whole, so the article on chayim makes both words definite.",hint:null},{prompt:"Why is it תורת משה and not תורה משה?",answer:"Because torah is feminine and ends in he, and in the construct state that ending becomes tav. The phrase is torat Mosheh, 'the Torah of Moses', at Joshua 8:31.",hint:"One letter changes, and it shows even unpointed."},{prompt:"Give two defensible translations of נתיבות פליאות חכמה, and say why it is ambiguous.",answer:"'Wondrous paths of wisdom' and 'paths of the wonders of wisdom'. The chain sets three nouns side by side without stating how they relate, so peliot may qualify netivot or may itself stand in construct with chokhmah. Both are printed; neither can be ruled out.",hint:"Where does the middle word attach?"},{prompt:"What do the two words of אין סוף mean separately?",answer:"Ein is the construct form of ayin, 'there is not, nothing'; sof is 'end'. Together, 'without end' — the Infinite. It is a coinage of the twelfth- and thirteenth-century kabbalists, not a biblical phrase.",hint:null},{prompt:"In the phrase על פני תהום, which noun is in the construct state, which is the absolute, and what does the phrase say literally?",answer:"Penei, the construct plural of panim, is the construct; tehom, 'deep', is the absolute, and carries no article. Panim is plural in form though singular in sense, so the phrase says literally 'upon the faces of a deep'.",hint:null}]},{number:6,title:"Counting the Letters",aim:"Reckon gematria correctly on real cases, and know honestly what the several methods are and what the technique can and cannot claim.",sections:[{heading:"Every letter a number",paragraphs:["Hebrew has no separate numerals. Aleph to tet are the units, yod to tsadi the tens, qof to tav the hundreds. There is no zero and no place value: a numeral is simply the sum of its letters, largest first, right to left as the script runs. A geresh after a lone letter, and gershayim before the last letter of a longer numeral, mark letters meant as number, as in תרי״ג for 613. There is one settled exception: fifteen and sixteen are written טו, nine and six, and טז, nine and seven, rather than יה and יו, so as not to write out the letters of the Name.","That every word is also a number is a fact of the writing system, not a doctrine. Reckoning a word's value and comparing it with another is gematria — usually derived from the Greek geometria — and it is old: at Nedarim 32a the 318 men Abram leads out at Genesis 14:14 are identified with Eliezer alone, since אליעזר sums to exactly that. In ordinary reckoning the five finals keep their plain values, a final mem being forty; the later convention giving them 500 to 900 is a distinct method, never to be applied silently."],examples:[]},{heading:"Cases worth knowing",paragraphs:["Work these by hand rather than reading them off. Chai, living, is chet 8 plus yod 10, eighteen — hence gifts in multiples of eighteen, the sum read as a blessing rather than a quantity. The Tetragrammaton is 10, 5, 6 and 5: twenty-six. Echad, one, is thirteen; ahavah, love, is also thirteen; together twenty-six, the Name. Be exact about provenance: that equation is a commonplace of later kabbalistic and Hasidic homily, not a Talmudic teaching, and is offered as a hint, a remez.","Taryag is 613, the commandments as reckoned by Rabbi Simlai at Makkot 23b–24a — 365 prohibitions and 248 positive commands, which sum to 613. The same passage notes that torah comes to 611: that many came through Moses, the first two of the Decalogue being heard directly. Elohim is 86, and ha-teva, nature, is also 86. Shaddai is 314, and so is the spelling מטטרון, Metatron; nachash, serpent, and mashiach both come to 358. The arithmetic in each case is plain and checkable; what anyone builds on it is a further question."],examples:[{native:"חי",translit:"ḥai",gloss:"living — 18",note:"Chet 8 + yod 10."},{native:"יהוה",translit:"YHWH",gloss:"the Name — 26",note:"10 + 5 + 6 + 5."},{native:"אחד",translit:"ʾeḥad",gloss:"one — 13",note:"1 + 8 + 4."},{native:"אהבה",translit:"ʾahavah",gloss:"love — 13",note:"1 + 5 + 2 + 5. With echad, 26."},{native:"תריג",translit:"taryag",gloss:"613",note:"400 + 200 + 10 + 3."},{native:"תורה",translit:"torah",gloss:"Torah — 611",note:"400 + 6 + 200 + 5; plus two heard directly, Makkot 23b–24a."},{native:"אלהים",translit:"ʾElohim",gloss:"God — 86",note:"1 + 30 + 5 + 10 + 40."},{native:"הטבע",translit:"ha-ṭevaʿ",gloss:"nature — 86",note:"5 + 9 + 2 + 70."},{native:"אליעזר",translit:"ʾEliʿezer",gloss:"Eliezer — 318",note:"Nedarim 32a on Genesis 14:14."}]},{heading:"The methods, and their honest limits",paragraphs:["Gematria is not one thing but a family of reckonings, and a responsible reader names which is in use. Mispar hechrachi, the absolute value, is the plain sum you have been practising and is the default. Mispar gadol gives the five finals 500 to 900. Mispar siduri numbers the letters 1 to 22 by position. Mispar katan strips the zeros from each value, so tav counts 4 and yod 1, making chai nine. Notarikon, which reads a word as the initials of a phrase, and temurah, which substitutes letters — as in the atbash by which Jeremiah 25:26 writes Babel as Sheshakh — are not gematria at all.","Now the honest part. Gematria demonstrates nothing. Any large stock of words throws up equalities by arithmetic necessity, and a reckoner free among five methods, or free to add the letter-count, can reach almost any total he wishes. In rabbinic usage a gematria is an asmakhta, a support hung on a teaching already held on other grounds. When a homilist shows that echad and ahavah both come to thirteen he is not proving that God is love; he is saying, in the language of the letters, that Israel's confession of unity and its command to love are one thing. That is worth hearing. It is not an argument."],examples:[]}],vocabulary:[{native:"גימטריה",translit:"gimaṭriyya",gloss:"gematria, reckoning by letter-value"},{native:"חי",translit:"ḥai",gloss:"living, alive"},{native:"אהבה",translit:"ʾahavah",gloss:"love"},{native:"אחד",translit:"ʾeḥad",gloss:"one"},{native:"תריג",translit:"taryag",gloss:"613, the commandments"},{native:"טבע",translit:"ṭevaʿ",gloss:"nature"},{native:"משיח",translit:"mashiaḥ",gloss:"anointed one, messiah"},{native:"מספר",translit:"mispar",gloss:"number"}],exercises:[{prompt:"Reckon the value of שלום by the absolute method.",answer:"376. Shin 300 + lamed 30 + vav 6 + final mem 40. The final form counts as forty, exactly as an ordinary mem does, unless mispar gadol has been announced.",hint:"Do not let the final mem mislead you."},{prompt:"Reckon the value of אמת.",answer:"441. Aleph 1 + mem 40 + tav 400.",hint:null},{prompt:"Reckon תורה, and say what Makkot 23b–24a makes of the shortfall from 613.",answer:"611. Tav 400 + vav 6 + resh 200 + he 5. The tradition explains that 611 commandments came through Moses, the first two of the Decalogue being heard directly: 611 plus 2 is 613.",hint:null},{prompt:"Reckon אחד and אהבה, and say what the tradition draws from the pair — and with what standing.",answer:"Each comes to 13: echad is 1 + 8 + 4, ahavah 1 + 5 + 2 + 5. Together 26, the value of the Tetragrammaton. The observation is a commonplace of later kabbalistic and Hasidic homily rather than a Talmudic teaching, and is offered as a hint, not a proof.",hint:null},{prompt:"Give the mispar katan of חי, and explain the method.",answer:"9. In mispar katan each letter's value is reduced by dropping its zeros: chet is already 8, and yod, ten, becomes 1. So 8 + 1 = 9, where the absolute value of the same word is 18.",hint:null},{prompt:"A correspondent sends you a gematria he says proves a doctrine. What should you reply?",answer:"That a gematria proves nothing by itself. There are several methods — absolute, great, ordinal, small — and a reckoner free among them, and free to add the letter-count, can reach a great many totals. In rabbinic usage a gematria is an asmakhta, a support for a teaching already held on other grounds. Ask what the teaching rests on apart from the number.",hint:null}]},{number:7,title:"Verses Entire",aim:"Read Genesis 1:1–3 word by word, with every prefix and construct now visible, and meet the untranslatable particle et.",sections:[{heading:"Genesis 1:1",paragraphs:["בראשית ברא אלהים את השמים ואת הארץ. Seven words and twenty-eight letters, both multiples of seven — a count later kabbalistic writing returns to often.","Bereshit you took apart in Lesson 4. Bara is he created, a qal perfect of bet-resh-aleph; in the qal Scripture uses this verb of no subject but God. Elohim is plural in form yet takes a singular verb here, a plural of majesty — real Hebrew, not a scribal slip. Then את, et: a particle placed before a definite direct object. English has nothing for it, so it goes untranslated. The kabbalists did not pass over it — spelled aleph-tav, the first letter and the last, it is read as the whole alphabet folded into the act of creation. That is a fine homily, and it is not what the grammar says. Ha-shamayim is article plus shamayim; the ending -ayim looks like a dual, as in mayim, and grammarians disagree whether it truly is one."],examples:[{native:"בראשית",translit:"bereʾshit",gloss:"in beginning",note:null},{native:"ברא",translit:"baraʾ",gloss:"he created",note:"In the qal, used of God alone."},{native:"אלהים",translit:"ʾElohim",gloss:"God",note:"Plural in form, singular here."},{native:"את",translit:"ʾet",gloss:"(marker of the definite object)",note:"Aleph and tav — first and last."}]},{heading:"Genesis 1:2",paragraphs:["והארץ היתה תהו ובהו וחשך על פני תהום ורוח אלהים מרחפת על פני המים. Fourteen words, and every structure of this course is in them.","Ve-ha-arets is two prefixes on a known noun. Haytah, she was, agrees with the feminine erets. Tohu va-vohu, formlessness and emptiness, occurs as a phrase only here and at Jeremiah 4:23, and bohu never appears in Scripture except beside tohu. Al penei is the preposition with the construct plural of panim: upon the faces of. Tehom, the deep, has no article — a common Semitic word, and the old claim that it is borrowed directly from the goddess Tiamat is not now accepted, though the two words are cognate. Ve-ruach Elohim is the construct chain of Lesson 5. Merachefet is a feminine piel participle, hovering; its only other piel is at Deuteronomy 32:11, of an eagle over its young — which is why the older versions choose brooding rather than the sweeping of a storm-wind."],examples:[{native:"והארץ",translit:"ve-ha-ʾarets",gloss:"and the earth",note:"ve- + ha- + erets."},{native:"היתה",translit:"haytah",gloss:"she was",note:null},{native:"תהו ובהו",translit:"tohu va-vohu",gloss:"formless and void",note:"The phrase occurs only here and Jeremiah 4:23."},{native:"וחשך",translit:"ve-ḥoshekh",gloss:"and darkness",note:"The first vav is the conjunction; the o is written defectively, with no vav of its own."},{native:"על פני",translit:"ʿal penei",gloss:"upon the face of",note:"Construct plural of panim."},{native:"תהום",translit:"tehom",gloss:"the deep",note:null},{native:"ורוח אלהים",translit:"ve-ruaḥ ʾElohim",gloss:"and the spirit of God; and a mighty wind",note:"Genuinely ambiguous."},{native:"מרחפת",translit:"meraḥefet",gloss:"hovering, fluttering",note:"Compare Deuteronomy 32:11."}]},{heading:"Genesis 1:3, and what you can now see",paragraphs:["ויאמר אלהים יהי אור ויהי אור. Six words. Va-yomer, and he said, with the vav-consecutive of Lesson 4. Elohim. Yehi, let there be, a jussive of he-yod-he. Or, light. Va-yehi, and there was, the same verb with the same reversing vav. Then or once more.","Read it aloud and hear what no translation quite carries: yehi or, va-yehi or. The command and its fulfilment are the same two words, the second with a single letter added. Look back at what you have done: segmented prefixes, identified roots, recognised construct chains, found a particle with no English equivalent, and noticed where the Hebrew is ambiguous and where a translator has chosen for you."],examples:[{native:"ויאמר",translit:"va-yoʾmer",gloss:"and he said",note:"Vav-consecutive."},{native:"יהי",translit:"yehi",gloss:"let there be",note:"Jussive of h-y-h."},{native:"ויהי",translit:"va-yehi",gloss:"and there was",note:"The same verb, reversed."}]}],vocabulary:[{native:"ברא",translit:"baraʾ",gloss:"he created"},{native:"את",translit:"ʾet",gloss:"marker of the definite object"},{native:"שמים",translit:"shamayim",gloss:"heavens, sky"},{native:"תהו",translit:"tohu",gloss:"formlessness, waste"},{native:"בהו",translit:"bohu",gloss:"emptiness, void"},{native:"חשך",translit:"ḥoshekh",gloss:"darkness"},{native:"תהום",translit:"tehom",gloss:"the deep, the abyss"},{native:"מרחפת",translit:"meraḥefet",gloss:"hovering (fem. participle)"}],exercises:[{prompt:"How many words and how many letters are there in Genesis 1:1?",answer:"Seven words and twenty-eight letters. Both are multiples of seven.",hint:"The words run 6, 3, 5, 2, 5, 3, 4 letters."},{prompt:"What does את do grammatically, and what have the kabbalists made of it?",answer:"Grammatically it is a particle marking a definite direct object, with no English equivalent, and is left untranslated. Because it is spelled aleph-tav, the first and last letters, kabbalistic reading takes it as the whole alphabet folded into the act of creation — a homily laid over the grammar, not the grammar itself.",hint:null},{prompt:"Reckon the gematria of בראשית by the absolute method.",answer:"913. Bet 2 + resh 200 + aleph 1 + shin 300 + yod 10 + tav 400.",hint:null},{prompt:"In ורוח אלהים, which word stands in construct, and give two defensible translations.",answer:"Ruach stands in construct with Elohim. It may be rendered 'and the spirit of God' or 'and a mighty wind', since Elohim can serve as a superlative; 'a wind from God' is a third defensible reading, and all are printed.",hint:null},{prompt:"Which word of Genesis 1:2 is a participle, and where else does its verb appear in the same binyan?",answer:"Merachefet, 'hovering, fluttering', a feminine piel participle. Its only other piel in the Bible is at Deuteronomy 32:11, of an eagle fluttering over its young.",hint:null}]},{number:8,title:"Hear, O Israel",aim:"Read the Shema with its enlarged letters and the opening mishnah of Sefer Yetzirah clause by clause, then take honest stock of what you can and cannot yet do.",sections:[{heading:"The six words of the Shema",paragraphs:["שמע ישראל יהוה אלהינו יהוה אחד — Deuteronomy 6:4. Six words, and you can read all of them. Shema is an imperative of shin-mem-ayin, to hear, and also to heed and obey; the English Hear is thinner than the Hebrew. Yisrael is a vocative. Then the Tetragrammaton, which is not pronounced — the reader says Adonai in its place — whose letters sum to twenty-six. Eloheinu is Elohim with the possessive suffix -einu, our. Then the Name again, and echad, one, worth thirteen.","Notice what is absent: there is no verb to be. Hebrew simply sets the words side by side, and the translator must decide where to put the English is. The LORD our God, the LORD is one is defensible; so is The LORD is our God, the LORD is one.","Two letters are written large in every Torah scroll: the ayin ending shema and the dalet ending echad. The enlargement belongs to the scribal tradition recorded in the masorah; the reasons given are later, and there are two. The plain one is protective — a dalet misread as a resh would turn echad, one, into acher, another. The homiletic one is that the enlarged letters spell עד, ed, witness. Both circulate, neither is stated in the biblical text, and both are worth knowing as what they are. At Berakhot 13b Rabbi Judah the Prince passes his hand over his eyes as he recites the verse, whence the custom of covering the eyes."],examples:[{native:"שמע",translit:"shemaʿ",gloss:"hear! heed!",note:"Its ayin is written large."},{native:"ישראל",translit:"Yisraʾel",gloss:"O Israel",note:null},{native:"יהוה",translit:"YHWH",gloss:"the LORD (read as Adonai)",note:"Not pronounced. Value 26."},{native:"אלהינו",translit:"ʾEloheinu",gloss:"our God",note:"Elohim with the suffix -einu."},{native:"אחד",translit:"ʾeḥad",gloss:"one",note:"Value 13. Its dalet is written large."},{native:"עד",translit:"ʿed",gloss:"witness",note:"The two enlarged letters together."}]},{heading:"With thirty-two paths",paragraphs:["Here is the whole opening mishnah of Sefer Yetzirah as the Gra–Ari recension prints it. בשלשים ושתים נתיבות פליאות חכמה חקק יה יהוה צבאות אלהי ישראל אלהים חיים ומלך עולם אל שדי רחום וחנון רם ונשא שוכן עד וקדוש שמו מרום וקדוש הוא וברא את עולמו בשלשה ספרים בספר וספר וספור. With thirty-two wondrous paths of wisdom, Yah, the LORD of hosts, the God of Israel, the living God and King of the world, El Shaddai, merciful and gracious, high and lofty, dwelling for ever, and holy is His name — He engraved, and created His world with three books: with sefer, sefar and sippur.","Take the first clause word by word. Bi-shloshim is be- on shloshim, thirty — the same bet that opens the Torah, here meaning by means of. U-shtayim is the conjunction in its u- shape, taken before a shin pointed with a sheva, on shtayim, two. Netivot is the plural of nativ, a path; peliot, wondrous; chokhmah, wisdom. Those three form the construct chain of Lesson 5, and the ambiguity is real: wondrous paths of wisdom, or paths of the wonders of wisdom. Chaqaq is engraved, incised, cut in — a stonecutter's verb, not a writer's, and the choice is the doctrine of the book in one word. Yah is the short Name, worth fifteen, which is why fifteen is written טו and not יה.","The titles between the verb and its object are quarried from Scripture: rachum ve-chanun from Exodus 34:6, ram ve-nissa shokhen ad ve-qadosh shemo from Isaiah 57:15. Then the three sefarim, spelled בספר וספר וספור: the first two are identical in consonants and distinguished only by pointing, sefer, writing, and sefar, number, with sippur, telling, third. All three are the root samekh-pe-resh of Lesson 3. What are the thirty-two paths? The next mishnah answers: עשר ספירות בלימה ועשרים ושתים אותיות יסוד, ten sefirot of nothingness and twenty-two foundation letters — ten and twenty-two make thirty-two.","One honesty to carry away. Sefer Yetzirah survives in several genuinely divergent versions — short, long, Saadia, and the much later Gra–Ari — whose chapter and verse divisions do not agree, which is why citations to it are only ever approximate; even within the Gra–Ari text the string of divine titles above varies somewhat between printings. It was first printed at Mantua in 1562, in more than one recension in the same volume. Its date is disputed: most scholarship places it between the third and sixth centuries, with proposals running from the second as late as the ninth."],examples:[{native:"בשלשים ושתים נתיבות פליאות חכמה חקק יה",translit:"bi-shloshim u-shtayim netivot peliʾot ḥokhmah ḥaqaq Yah",gloss:"With thirty-two wondrous paths of wisdom Yah engraved",note:"Sefer Yetzirah 1:1, opening clause, Gra–Ari recension."},{native:"בשלשים",translit:"bi-shloshim",gloss:"with thirty",note:"be- + shloshim."},{native:"ושתים",translit:"u-shtayim",gloss:"and two",note:"The conjunction in its u- shape."},{native:"נתיבות",translit:"netivot",gloss:"paths",note:"Plural of nativ."},{native:"פליאות",translit:"peliʾot",gloss:"wonders, wondrous",note:null},{native:"חכמה",translit:"ḥokhmah",gloss:"wisdom",note:null},{native:"חקק",translit:"ḥaqaq",gloss:"he engraved, incised",note:"A stonecutter's verb."},{native:"יה",translit:"Yah",gloss:"Yah, the short Name",note:"Yod 10 + he 5 = 15."},{native:"בספר וספר וספור",translit:"be-sefer ve-sefar ve-sippur",gloss:"with writing, number and telling",note:"The first two differ only in pointing."},{native:"עשר ספירות בלימה ועשרים ושתים אותיות יסוד",translit:"ʿeser sefirot belimah ve-ʿesrim u-shtayim ʾotiyyot yesod",gloss:"ten sefirot of nothingness and twenty-two foundation letters",note:"Sefer Yetzirah 1:2 — the thirty-two named."}]},{heading:"What you can read, and what you cannot yet",paragraphs:["Eight lessons ago you could not tell a dalet from a resh. You can now open a Hebrew page, find the root under a word, strip its prefixes, recognise a construct chain and know where it is ambiguous, reckon a gematria by a named method, and read two of the tradition's weightiest sentences in the original. Take the hall's other readings next and work them as you worked Genesis 1.","Be equally clear about what you have not learned. The verbal system has barely been opened: seven binyanim, perfect and imperfect, participles, weak roots, suffixed forms. The accents that govern chanting were not touched, nor the syntax of longer sentences. And the Zohar is largely in Aramaic — a cousin language with its own grammar, learnable from here but not identical with what you have.","One last counsel. The tradition you are entering reads with enormous freedom, and that freedom is worth something only when it rests on exact knowledge of what the letters say. Learn the plain sense first and hold it steadily; then the homily, the number and the secret have something to be secrets of. A reader who cannot construe the verse has nothing to interpret, and a reader who will not let the verse resist him is not interpreting at all."],examples:[]}],vocabulary:[{native:"שמע",translit:"shemaʿ",gloss:"hear! heed!"},{native:"ישראל",translit:"Yisraʾel",gloss:"Israel"},{native:"אלהינו",translit:"ʾEloheinu",gloss:"our God"},{native:"עד",translit:"ʿed",gloss:"witness"},{native:"שלשים",translit:"shloshim",gloss:"thirty"},{native:"נתיבות",translit:"netivot",gloss:"paths"},{native:"פליאות",translit:"peliʾot",gloss:"wonders, wondrous"},{native:"חכמה",translit:"ḥokhmah",gloss:"wisdom"}],exercises:[{prompt:"Write out the six words of the Shema in order, right to left, with a gloss for each.",answer:"שמע shema, 'hear'; ישראל Yisrael, 'O Israel'; יהוה YHWH, the Name, read as Adonai; אלהינו eloheinu, 'our God'; יהוה YHWH again; אחד echad, 'one'.",hint:null},{prompt:"Which two letters of the Shema are written large, what do they spell, and what is the other reason given for the enlargement?",answer:"The ayin ending shema and the dalet ending echad. Together they spell עד, ed, 'witness'. The other and plainer reason is protective: an enlarged dalet cannot be misread as a resh, which would turn echad, 'one', into acher, 'another'.",hint:null},{prompt:"Why does the Shema contain no word meaning 'is', and what follows for translators?",answer:"Because Hebrew forms such sentences by simple juxtaposition, with no copula. The translator must decide where to insert the English 'is', and more than one placement is defensible: 'the LORD our God, the LORD is one' and 'the LORD is our God, the LORD is one' both render the same six words.",hint:null},{prompt:"Take בשלשים ושתים apart into its pieces.",answer:"Bi-, the prefix be- meaning 'with, by means of'; shloshim, 'thirty'; u-, the conjunction 'and' in the shape it takes before a shin pointed with a sheva; shtayim, 'two'. With thirty-two.",hint:null},{prompt:"What are the thirty-two paths, and where does Sefer Yetzirah say so?",answer:"The ten sefirot and the twenty-two letters of the alphabet: ten plus twenty-two is thirty-two. The book states it in the following mishnah, numbered 1:2 in the Gra–Ari recension — עשר ספירות בלימה ועשרים ושתים אותיות יסוד, 'ten sefirot of nothingness and twenty-two foundation letters'.",hint:"Add the two things this course has been about."},{prompt:"Why does the mishnah use חקק rather than a verb of writing?",answer:"Because chaqaq means to engrave, incise or cut in — a stonecutter's word. The book is not saying God wrote the world down but that He cut the letters into something, which is why its language throughout is of engraving, hewing and permuting rather than of composition.",hint:null}]}]};export{e as default};
